---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../components/PageIntro.astro";
import CubeRow from "../../components/CubeRow.astro";
import { foods, categories, foodPath, fmt1, TEASPOON_GL } from "../../lib/foods";

const groups = categories.map((c) => ({ ...c, items: foods.filter((f) => f.category === c.id) }));
const zeroCount = foods.filter((f) => f.cubes === 0).length;
const perHundred = foods.filter((f) => /^100(g|ml)\b/.test(f.portion)).length;
---

<Layout
  title={`Teaspoons of sugar in ${foods.length} foods, by category`}
  description={`Every food in the sugar teaspoon calculator grouped by category, with its portion and teaspoon-of-sugar equivalent, each linked to the figures and source behind it.`}
>
  <PageIntro
    slot="intro"
    title="Teaspoons of sugar, food by food"
    lede={`All ${foods.length} foods in the calculator, grouped by category. Each figure is the number of 4g teaspoons of table sugar with about the same effect on blood glucose as the portion shown.`}
  />

  <section class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-10 sm:px-6 sm:py-14">
    <nav aria-label="Categories">
      <ul class="flex flex-wrap gap-2">
        {
          groups.map((g) => (
            <li>
              <a class="tab" href={`#${g.id}`}>
                {g.label}
              </a>
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ul>
    </nav>

    {
      groups.map((g) => (
        <section id={g.id} class="mt-10 scroll-mt-4" aria-labelledby={`${g.id}-heading`}>
          <h2 id={`${g.id}-heading`} class="font-serif text-2xl font-medium text-ink">
            {g.label}
          </h2>
          <ul class="mt-4 divide-y divide-line rounded-md border border-line bg-paper">
            {g.items.map((f) => (
              <li>
                <a href={foodPath(f)} class="group flex min-h-[44px] flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-x-4 gap-y-2 px-4 py-3 hover:bg-warm">
                  <span class="min-w-0">
                    <span class="block font-semibold leading-snug text-ink group-hover:text-green">{f.name}</span>
                    <span class="block text-sm leading-snug text-muted">{f.portion}</span>
                  </span>
                  <span class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
                    <CubeRow count={f.cubes} class="hidden sm:flex" />
                    <span class="flex items-baseline gap-1.5">
                      <span class="font-serif text-xl text-coral">{fmt1(f.cubes)}</span>
                      <span class="text-sm text-muted">teaspoons</span>
                    </span>
                  </span>
                </a>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      ))
    }

    <div class="prose-body mt-14">
      <h2 style="margin-top:0">What each figure is</h2>
      <p>
        The figure next to each food answers one question: how many 4g teaspoons of table sugar would raise blood glucose by
        about as much as that portion? It is not the "sugars" line from a nutrition label. A label counts sugars as a
        chemical group and leaves starch out, even though digestion turns starch into glucose. The teaspoon figure covers all
        the digestible carbohydrate in the portion, whether it started as sugar or as starch.
      </p>
      <p>
        The method belongs to Dr David Unwin, Dr David Haslam and Dr Geoffrey Livesey, who published it in the Journal of
        Insulin Resistance in 2016. A food's glycaemic load is its glycaemic index multiplied by the grams of available
        carbohydrate in the portion, divided by 100. One teaspoon of table sugar has a glycaemic load of {TEASPOON_GL}, so a
        food's teaspoon figure is its glycaemic load divided by {TEASPOON_GL}. The page
        <a href="/what-is-glycemic-load/">what is glycaemic load</a> works through the arithmetic.
      </p>

      <h2>How to read the list</h2>
      <p>
        Read the portion before the number. Most portions are an ordinary serving with a gram weight: a 30g slice of bread, a
        150g bowl of rice, a 120g piece of fruit. {perHundred} foods are given per 100g or 100ml because that is how their
        source printed them, and 100g of honey is a larger amount than most people eat at once. Twice the portion is twice
        the figure.
      </p>
      <p>
        The categories follow the calculator's tabs. Within each category, foods are listed in the order of the calculator
        rather than by size of figure, so neighbouring rows are not a ranking. {zeroCount} foods carry a figure of zero,
        because their portion contains no measurable available carbohydrate or has a glycaemic index of zero.
      </p>

      <h2>What each food page shows</h2>
      <p>
        Each food links to a page of its own. That page gives the glycaemic index and the grams of carbohydrate where the
        source printed them, the glycaemic load, and the arithmetic that leads to the teaspoon figure. It names the chart or
        table the figure was read from, notes where two published charts give different figures for the same food, and sets
        the food beside two or three others from the list.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every value comes from Dr David Unwin's charts, distributed free by the Public Health Collaboration at
        <a href="https://phcuk.org/sugar">phcuk.org/sugar</a> and used with PHC's permission, from Table 1 of the
        2016 paper, or, for three meats and fish, from a carbohydrate content of zero in USDA FoodData Central. No value was
        estimated to fill a gap. The complete record, including foods that were left out and why, is on the
        <a href="/sources/">sources page</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The figures are averages from published tables, and individual blood glucose responses vary. They are educational
        estimates, not medical or dietary advice. To add several foods together, use the <a href="/">calculator</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
